medical-debt ad fight sustain Hamilton's polling surge through the final weeks, or whether Kansas's partisan baseline reasserts itself.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Roger Marshall (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Kansas Senate features incumbent Republican Roger Marshall against Democrat Adam Hamilton in a state that has not elected a Democratic senator since 1932. Marshall holds a large cash advantage and the state's partisan lean favors him, yet the race has tightened dramatically: recent September polls from Emerson and Wedgewood show Hamilton narrowly ahead, and averages put the contest near even.\n\nThe race gained attention after a former Republican governor and one of Marshall's primary rivals endorsed Hamilton, and national outlets describe GOP anxiety about an upset. Still, prediction markets give Marshall roughly a two-thirds chance, and most expert ratings sit at Lean R. The race is genuinely competitive but Marshall retains the edge.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.2893, "margin": -2.76}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.3585, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.4222, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.4301, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.3024, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2988, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.4183, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.4225, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.3457, "margin": -2.5}]}